| Emitting a particle is not filling in a form. A handler is never gated on
| the fields it happens to declare: a field the particle does not carry
| reads as null, exactly as `.field` does everywhere else in the language,
| and the handler runs and answers on that basis.
|
| Owner's rule, 2026-08-28: "handlerları çağırabilmek için bir validasyon
| yapmasını istemiyorum". `net` was rewritten around it in phase 2; core and
| the other three modules caught up here.
link "native_modules/strings.so" as strings
link "native_modules/math.so" as math
link "native_modules/console.so" as con

| A user-defined handler: `who` is simply null, and the body decides.
Greet { who } =>
    return Greeting { missing = who = null }
emit Greet {} to this get g
assert g.missing = true

| Null has no text length; omission and explicit null have one diagnostic.
emit Length {} to strings get missing_length
emit Length { value = null } to strings get null_length
assert missing_length ∈ Exception
assert missing_length.message = null_length.message

| A module handler with nothing to validate answers rather than refusing:
| there is no value `Echo` cannot pass through, or `Print` cannot render.
emit Echo {} to strings get e
assert e ∈ EchoResult
assert e.value = null

emit Print {} to con get p
assert p ∈ PrintResult
assert p.value = 4

| One that genuinely cannot proceed still fails — on the value, not on its
| absence. `Double { }` is `Double { value = null }`, and null is not a
| number. One message now, where there used to be two.
emit Double {} to math get d
assert d ∈ Exception
assert d.message = "Double requires a numeric 'value'"
